      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_12e41c90c7fb67440602311be8605db6ec9b414d5d6249c4a3cd48d981420611",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Finite-field geometry supplies concrete instances of roles that remain missing in the classical problem: curve point counts become Frobenius power sums, Weil cohomology carries products, duality, and cycle classes into finite-dimensional linear algebra, and a positive polarization can turn geometric scaling into an eigenvalue-modulus constraint. These examples specify roles for a hoped-for global mechanism without supplying one.",
